entries.english:
Friedrich Karl [von Schönborn], Bischof zu Bamberg und Würzburg, grants the brothers and sons of Anton Philibert von Rosenbach—Johann Philipp, Domherr in Würzburg, acting for himself and as representative for his absent brother Lothar Josef Heinrich, Domherr in Mainz and Würzburg; Adalbert Friedrich, bischöflicher Rat und Amtmann zu Arnstein; and Karl Christoph von Rosenberg—the following properties as male fiefs:
- Thundorf Castle, with all rights, appurtenances, and revenues, except only the farmstead;
- the deserted settlement of Ransbach, with its mill and farmstead, and all rights and revenues;
- the villages of Theinfeld and Rothhausen, with all rights, possessions, and revenues there;
- an estate in Maßbach behind the churchyard;
- the estates in Poppenlauer;
- the estates in Ebertshausen;
- half of the tithe at Altenmünster;
- one third of the tithe at Oberlauringen;
- one eighth of the tithe at Alsleben;
- one quarter of the tithe at Ober- and Untereßfeld;
- a vineyard and two wooded plots at Thundorf.

Furthermore, the bishop grants them the following properties, which had belonged outright to Veit Ulrich von Schaumberg and which he then transferred to the Würzburg bishopric as fiefs:
- the farmstead and vineyards at Thundorf;
- the lake at Weißensee;
- a fishing right in the Lauer below Maßbach;
- a fishing right in the Lauer within the Rothhausen district;
- three quarters of the mill at Rothhausen;
- two estates at Theinfeld;
- two freehold estates at Weichtungen;
- four rent fiefs at Stadtlauringen;
- nine estates and eleven fiefs in Maßbach;
- the Brandmühle below Maßbach;
- five estates and four fiefs in Poppenlauer;
- three estates and one fief in Nüdlingen;
- one fief at Rannungen;
- a field estate at Hesselbach;
- the Herlingsberg wooded plot;
- the male fief at Prappach.
These properties were purchased from the brothers Georg Dietrich, Wilhelm Ludwig, Wilhelm Ulrich, Georg Wilhelm, Johann Sigmund, Johann Philipp, and Johann Wilhelm von Schaumberg.

Finally, the bishop also grants them the following properties, which originally were fiefs of the County of Henneberg and then of the Duchy of Saxe-Weimar, and which passed to Würzburg under an agreement between the Würzburg bishopric and the Duchy of Saxe-Weimar:
- the high criminal court at Maßbach, subsequently moved to Poppenlauer, with all appurtenances and rights, excluding the villages of Maßbach and Volkershausen;
- half of the bailiwick at Poppenlauer;
- two estates in Maßbach and a meadow between Maßbach and Rothhausen;
- one quarter of the tithe at Zeil am Main;
- one third of one small quarter of the wine tithe at Zeil am Main;
- one third and one quarter of one full quarter of the wine tithe at Zeil am Main;
- four wooded plots near Maßbach;
- the wine rents and interest revenues at Schonungen. If the von Rosenbach family should ever have no male heirs, the Würzburg bishopric is obliged to pay the family's female heirs 2,000 Reichstaler in exchange for these revenues.

Sealed by: the bishop, with his secret seal.

Annotations: abstract (18th century).
